{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792925972,"nonce":"JmyUDdZ+pnS67nago+w+fQSfr3Q=","title":"Tiered: more compile workers over-speculate and oversubscribe the cores","message":"`ZYNTAX_COMPILE_WORKERS=\u003cn\u003e` runs n workers on the compile queue (default one). Cranelift's compile already runs outside the backend lock, and a tier-one compile's resume points now do too, so n workers generate code side by side. Measured on the NUC (taskset 0-3, release, ZYPY_LLVM=1, 11 interleaved reps, --fast mean relative to 39cdefea; the box was loaded by others during part of it, so spectral-norm, nbody and raytrace have wide quartiles):\n\n| kernel | 1 worker | 2 | 3 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| deltablue | 0.94 | 1.05 | 1.08 |\n| meteor-contest | 0.99 | 0.92 | 0.91 |\n| richards | 0.97 | 0.72 | 0.77 |\n| spectral-norm | 0.91 | 1.13 | 1.11 |\n\nWhy deltablue loses (ZYNTAX_TRACE_LAZY, workers 1 vs 3, taskset 0-3): blocking first-call compiles drop from about 80 to about 40, but their time does not (49 vs 45 to 50 ms), because each compile runs 1.7x longer; the workers compile far more (library \"finished\" 45 -\u003e 69..78, loop-free bodies 12 -\u003e 31, about 300 ms of compile CPU against 80). A compile ahead of the first call queues its own callees with no count, which is never stale, so more workers walk the transitive callee closure while the program runs, on the same four cores. With 8 or 12 cores the per-compile times stay about 2x as well: the backend lock (translation and installation) and the scratch and cell waits in optimize_body serialise them; the warm-up threads' lock wait went 11 -\u003e 72 ms.\n\nOptions, from what the trace shows:\n1. Speculation by depth: a compile made only because a compiled caller may call the function does not queue its callees, or queues them behind every depth-one request.\n2. Compile a function's direct callees before publishing it (a batch per function, forked over the workers and joined), so its native code meets no stub; the function stays interpreted meanwhile. This is Rayzor's batch compile scoped to one caller.\n3. Run a loop-free function's first call from native code in the interpreter while a worker compiles it (Rayzor's trampolines): deltablue has 53 such blocking compiles (23 ms of the 49). Needs a native-to-interpreter entry per signature, which does not exist yet.","files":null}]}